course: money
lesson: 1
title: What money is and why people accept it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Money is anything that everybody agrees to swap for things, and today that means coins, notes and numbers in a bank.
explain: A £5 note is only a bit of paper or plastic, yet the sweet shop takes it for five £1 bags of sweets, because the shopkeeper knows the next shop, the baker and the bus driver will all take it from her too.
explain: A shiny button or a brilliant yo-yo is not money, because nobody has agreed to treat it as money; money works only while people trust it.
explain: Money does three jobs: it buys things, it keeps its value so you can save it for later, and it tells you what things cost so you can compare them.
explain: Money on its own is worth nothing to eat or play with; it is a tool for trading, and it is only useful when other people have things to sell.
fact: Money is something everyone agrees to swap for goods and work.
fact: A note or coin is worth what it says because people trust that everyone else will accept it too.
fact: Without money you would have to find someone who has what you want and also wants exactly what you have.
fact: Money has three jobs: you spend it, you save it for later, and you use it to compare prices.
fact: Money can be cash (coins and notes) or digital money (numbers kept by a bank and spent with a card or phone); both buy the same things.
fact: If a country prints far too much money while there are no more things to buy, prices go up and nobody is richer.
fact: Old coins and notes can stop being accepted in shops; some banks still swap them, and collectors may want rare ones.
fact: The same note can pay for many jobs in one day as it passes from person to person; this is called money circulating.
fact: Long ago a British note could be swapped at the bank for gold; today money is not backed by gold but by the government and people's trust.
fact: "Quid" is slang for a pound, a "fiver" is £5 and a "tenner" is £10.
fact: Money is only useful when someone has something to sell; a pile of coins on a desert island buys nothing.
exercise: Why does the sweet shop take a £5 note, which is just printed paper? | Everyone trusts that it can be spent again in other shops | The paper itself is very rare and costs a lot of money to buy | The note has a tiny bit of gold hidden inside it | Shops must take any paper that has a picture of a king
exercise: Bags of sweets cost £1 each. How many bags does a £5 note buy? | 5 bags | 4 bags | 6 bags | 10 bags
exercise: Ava has £1 and a yo-yo. The comic costs £3 and the man wants only money. How much more money does she need? | £2 | £3 | £1 | £4
exercise: Why does the comic shop man want money and not Ava's yo-yo? | He can spend money on anything he needs, like bread or bus fares | Yo-yos are against the law in comic shops | A yo-yo is worth much more than a comic, so it is unfair | Money is heavier, so it is harder to steal from him
exercise: A little girl gives the ice-cream man a shiny button. Why isn't it money? | Nobody has agreed to accept buttons as money | It is not round enough or heavy enough to count as a coin | It is too small and too light to be worth anything | Buttons only count as money in other countries
exercise: Ice cream costs $2. Kai pays with $1 coins. How many coins does he hand over? | 2 coins | 1 coin | 3 coins | 4 coins
exercise: What are the three jobs of money? | To buy things, to keep value for later, and to show what things cost | To buy things, to look shiny, and to fill piggy banks | To pay taxes, to make gold, and to be printed | To buy sweets, to buy toys, and to buy games
exercise: A pencil costs 50p and a rubber 25p. How much are both together? | 75p | 70p | 80p | 65p
exercise: Josh's mum has £50 on her card and £5 in her purse. How much money does she have in all? | £55 | £45 | £50 | £60
exercise: If everyone's money doubled overnight but there was still the same number of cakes, what would happen? | Cakes would cost about twice as much, so nobody would be richer | Everyone could buy twice as many cakes as before | Cakes would become free because there is so much money | Nothing at all, prices never change
exercise: Oscar takes an old coin from Gran's drawer to the shop. Why won't the shop take it? | That coin is no longer used as money in shops | The shop only takes notes, never coins of any kind | Old coins are always fakes made to fool people | The lady at the till did not like the look of it
exercise: The window cleaner, the baker and Dev's dad each get the same £10 note in turn for a job. How much work did that one note pay for? | £30 of work | £10 of work | £20 of work | £40 of work
exercise: Lily says she would buy everything if she had all the money in the world. What is the problem? | Money only buys things when other people have things to sell | She would need a bigger purse to carry it all | Shops close when one person has too much money | Money stops working on Sundays
exercise: What is money backed by today in countries like the UK? | The government and people's trust in it | A pile of gold for every note printed | The weight of the metal in each coin | The money in the king's private bank account
exercise: Josh says money is just numbers on a screen; Finn says it's coins. Who is right? | Both, because money can be cash or numbers in a bank | Only Josh, because cash has disappeared completely now | Only Finn, because screens cannot hold money | Neither, because real money is only gold
exercise: Oscar's lolly is 40p. He pays with 20p coins. How many does he need? | 2 | 4 | 3 | 1
test: Your mate's like "paper money's literally worth nothing lol, it's just paper". What's the best answer? | The paper is cheap, but everyone trusts it, so it buys real things | He's right, which is why shops only take coins | He's wrong, because notes have gold threads worth a fiver | He's right, but only since phones were invented
test: Gran goes "back in my day you could take a pound note to the bank and get gold for it, pet." Is that still true? | No, money today is backed by trust and the government, not gold | Yes, every bank still swaps notes for gold bars | Yes, but only on Mondays and only for grandparents | No, because gold was banned by law last year
test: A cartoon pirate buys a whole ship with one shiny button. What's wrong with this? | A button isn't money, because nobody agreed to take it as money | Nothing, buttons were money in pirate times | Pirates always paid with buttons, so it's correct | Ships can only be bought with yo-yos
test: Your cousin goes, "why don't the government just print a million quid for everyone, no cap?" ("No cap" means "honestly".) What would really happen? | Prices would shoot up, so a loaf might cost a fortune | Everyone would be a millionaire and buy anything they want | The money would turn into gold in the bank | Nothing, because printed money is never spent
test: What would happen if every shop suddenly stopped trusting £10 notes? | The notes would be almost useless for buying anything | The notes would be worth double the very next day instead | People would get free shopping with them for a whole week | Nothing, because notes are always worth the same amount
test: Ice cream's $2, she's got one $1 coin, then — no wait — two $1 coins. Can she pay? | Yes, $1 + $1 is exactly $2 | No, she is still $1 short | No, ice-cream vans only take notes | Yes, and she gets $1 change
test: Ollie reckons a pile of 100 coins on a desert island makes you rich. ("Reckons" means thinks.) Is he right? | No, with nobody selling anything the coins buy nothing | Yes, 100 coins will always buy 100 things wherever you are | Yes, because the metal coins can be eaten in an emergency | No, because islands only ever use paper money, not coins
test: A Geordie nan says "that's a canny fiver ye've got there, hinny." ("Canny" means nice; "hinny" means dear.) How much money is it? | £5 | £15 | 50p | £50
test: Dev's mate scoffs, "the same tenner can't pay three people, ya numpty." What's the truth? | It can, because the note passes from person to person | He's right, a note can only be spent once ever | He's right, unless the note is cut into three | It can, but only if a bank prints the note again each time
test: Your little bro tries to buy a pencil with "two rubbers' worth" of stickers. Why is money easier? | Money gives every thing a price you can compare straight away | Stickers are always worth more than pencils, so he'd lose out there | Shops are not allowed to look at stickers or take them as payment | Money is easier only because it is shinier and nicer than stickers
test: Uncle says, "Old coins are rubbish, chuck 'em." What's a better idea? | Ask a bank about swapping them, or keep rare ones for collectors | Spend them anyway, shops must take any coin | Melt them into new coins at home | Throw them in the river for luck
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the nature of money? | Money is any article universally accepted in exchange for goods and services | Money is any article possessing considerable weight and a bright, shiny appearance | Money is exclusively gold retained within a national vault | Money is any object which its owner personally regards as being of value
exam: It is given that confectionery is sold at one pound per bag. Determine the number of bags obtainable in exchange for a note of five pounds. | Five | Four | Six | Ten
exam: Identify the principal consequence of a government issuing a greatly increased quantity of money whilst the quantity of goods remains unchanged. | A general rise in prices, such that no person is made richer | A general fall in prices, such that all persons are made richer | The automatic conversion of all banknotes into gold | No alteration whatsoever in prices or in wealth
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the basis of the value of modern banknotes. | Their value rests upon public trust and the authority of the government | Their value rests upon the quantity of gold held in a vault for each note | Their value rests upon the cost of the paper and ink employed | Their value rests upon the personal wealth of the monarch
